description:
As mentioned by [~tnorimat] in the comment 
{code}
WebAuthn credential doesn't have any confidential data. If Attestation Statement itself is shown on an admin console, I'm not sure it is useful. the format of Attestation Statement might be useful. For example, if its format is "none", we can see that its webauthn authenticator is less credible than the one whose attestation statement format is "packed", "tpm", and so on.
{code}
We can add the attestation statement format as another property to WebAuthnCredentialData. That should ensure that the format will be displayed in the admin console in the "credentials" tab of some user as part of the "webauthn" credential.

full_text:
WebAuthn: show the attestation statement format in the admin console

As mentioned by [~tnorimat] in the comment 
{code}
WebAuthn credential doesn't have any confidential data. If Attestation Statement itself is shown on an admin console, I'm not sure it is useful. the format of Attestation Statement might be useful. For example, if its format is "none", we can see that its webauthn authenticator is less credible than the one whose attestation statement format is "packed", "tpm", and so on.
{code}
We can add the attestation statement format as another property to WebAuthnCredentialData. That should ensure that the format will be displayed in the admin console in the "credentials" tab of some user as part of the "webauthn" credential.

description:
New methods may be added to the Authenticator or maybe rather AuthenticatorFactory interface. Those methods may be needed for rendering the table where user can choose from various
alternative mechanisms for authentication during particular step. For example during 2-factor authentication, user will be able to select between OTP and WebAuthn. See slide 12 for more details: 
The new methods should be likely something like this:
{code}
/**
 * Returns the label, which will be shown to the user on the login screen with available authentication mechanisms. This label will reference this particular authenticator type.
 * It should be clear to end users. For example, implementations can return "Authenticator Application" for OTP or "Security Key" for WebAuthn.
 * Ideally this should be a message, so that it is possible to localize it for various languages
 *
 * Authenticators, which don't require user interactions may typically just fallback to getDisplayType
 */
default String getUserLabel() {
 return getDisplayType();
}
/**
 * Returns the help text, which will be shown to the user on the login screen with available authentication mechanisms in addition to the label. This label will reference this particular authenticator type.
 * For example for OTP, the returned text could be "Enter a verification code from authenticator application" as described in the design 
 *
 * Ideally this should be a message, so that it is possible to localize it for various languages
 *
 * Authenticators, which don't require user interactions may typically just fallback to getDisplayType
 */
default String getUserLabelHelpText() {
 return getDisplayType();
}
/**
 * Return the icon CSS to be displayed in the table when user can select from various possibilities
 */
default String getIconCss() {
 return null;
}
{code}
The alternative is to introduce single method on AuthenticatorFactory and create the class, which will encapsulate all 3 metadata fields described above. Something like:
{code}
default AuthenticationSelectionMetadata getAuthenticationSelectionMetadata() {
 // Null helpText and null icon by default
 return new AuthenticationSelectionMetadata(getDisplayType(), null, null);
}
{code}
The returned values may then need to be filled to the class AuthenticationSelectionOption, so that they can be consumed in the freemarker template select-authenticator.ftl

