description:
Currently IdentityProviderMapper.updateBrokeredUser is called during every broker login. I suppose that with new changes, it will be still always called for some modes?
For many mappers, this method usually updates something on the user even if current value is same. For example firstName is always updated to "John" even if current firstName in Keycloak DB is already "John" .
This results in:
- Unecessary DB SQL call to update user (or add/remove role etc)
- Unecessary removing of this user from the cache
I wonder if we want to address this as part of this work or as separate task? We can possibly do 2 things:
1) Update the code of the individual mappers to count with the previous value. For example instead of this: 
{code}
userModel.setFirstName(brokerFirstName);
{code} 
we can use something like this:
{code}
String currentKeycloakFirstName = userModel.getFirstName();
// Update model just if firstName not match 
if (currentKeycloakFirstName.equals(brokerFirstName)) { 
 userModel.setFirstName(brokerFirstName);
} 
{code}
2) Handle this at model level and ensure that update to the store is not propagated if same as previous value.
If we go with (1), then maybe we can address this as part of this work on Identity mappers refactoring? If we go with (2), we can possibly wait for the new store, but IMO will be good to make sure we don't forget to address this when working on the new store..
Per mail discussion, we may rather address this at model level to make it reusable in both IdentityProviderMapper and other places.

full_text:
