description:
1. Define the KeycloakAuthorizationRequest interface better than I did (I just put any type everywhere because I was not sure how stable the current interface in beta is).

2. make the init() function and KeycloakAuthorization() constructor function asynchronous and return observable/promise. Currently, they use synchronous(blocking) HTTP requests. I have only made one edit to keycloak-authz.js to at least return the reference to an instance when the KeycloakAuthorization is initialized. Before it returned nothing. 
3. export the keycloak-authz.js properly. Currently, when I want to import authorization from the npm package I have to import it as
{code}
import * as KeycloakAuthorization from 'keycloak-js/dist/keycloak-authz';
{code}
4. The new authorize() function could be improved. For example when this "if" expression

evaluates to false, then the promise does not return anything. There should probably be an "else" statement which calls onError or onDeny when no ticket is provided.

full_text:
Improvements to Keycloak AuthZ TypeScript Adapter

1. Define the KeycloakAuthorizationRequest interface better than I did (I just put any type everywhere because I was not sure how stable the current interface in beta is).

2. make the init() function and KeycloakAuthorization() constructor function asynchronous and return observable/promise. Currently, they use synchronous(blocking) HTTP requests. I have only made one edit to keycloak-authz.js to at least return the reference to an instance when the KeycloakAuthorization is initialized. Before it returned nothing. 
3. export the keycloak-authz.js properly. Currently, when I want to import authorization from the npm package I have to import it as
{code}
import * as KeycloakAuthorization from 'keycloak-js/dist/keycloak-authz';
{code}
4. The new authorize() function could be improved. For example when this "if" expression

evaluates to false, then the promise does not return anything. There should probably be an "else" statement which calls onError or onDeny when no ticket is provided.

description:
- There will be an options for adapters to dynamically register themselves to Keycloak. It's used for cluster setup, so when new application node joins the cluster, it will send 'registration' message to keycloak. The message will be also sent after specified period of time (periodic re-registration). So new options for adapters are:
-- register-node-at-startup (true/false)
-- register-node-period (Period in seconds. -1 means that periodic re-registration is disabled)
- There is endpoint at KC to handle those registration messages. Once new node sends registration message, the KC will:
-- authenticates the client who sent the message (functionality is not available to public clients. Just to "confidential" and "bearer-only")
-- updates the managementUrls for the specified node if it's not already there. Also it updates time from last re-registration
- Once the global event occurs (logoutAll, push notBefore) keycloak will send it to all dynamically registered nodes. (Note that if dynamic registration is not enabled, like for non-clustered setup, KC will send the message just to single statically managementUrl like it's now). Before sending the message, KC verifies that the period since last re-registration of node is not exceeded. Otherwise it removes the node from dynamically registered nodes and print warning to log. 
Period will be configurable per ApplicationModel (maxNodeRegistrationIdleTimeout) and should be same or (or better a bit higher) than register-node-period for corresponding adapter. 
Changes in ApplicationModel:
{code}
List<NodeRegistration> managementUris;
public int maxNodeRegistrationIdleTimeout;
public class NodeRegistration {
 private String nodeManagementUri;
 private int lastRegistrationTime;
}
{code}

