description:
Currently we have many places in the code with the pattern like:
{code}
UserSessionModel session = session.sessions().getUserSession(realm, sessionId);
// Fallback to offline
if (session == null) {
 session = session.sessions().getOfflineSession(realm, sessionId);
}
{code}
there are 2 issues:
1) The boilerplate code above
2) Not good performance as under the covers, we need to query both infinispan "sessions" cache and then fallback to "offlineSessions" cache. Which could mean unecessary cluster remote calls...
This is the issue usually when session_state in the accessToken/IDToken is verified as those tokens don't have information if session was "online" or "offline" session. We may add the flag to tokens to the session_state like this for normal sessions:
"session_state": "onl/12345"
or for offline sessions:
"session_state": "off/12345"
And have some common method on UserSessionManager, which will be able to translate this session_state and lookup either "sessions" or "offlineSessions". This should help with both (1) and (2) though.
Will probably need to change accessTokens/IDTokens this way. Not sure about refresh/offline tokens as those have information if session is online or offline (token type) and changing session_state may cause some issues and additional migration effort for backwards compatibility for offline tokens.

full_text:
Add onl/off flags to the session_state in tokens to improve lookup sessions

Currently we have many places in the code with the pattern like:
{code}
UserSessionModel session = session.sessions().getUserSession(realm, sessionId);
// Fallback to offline
if (session == null) {
 session = session.sessions().getOfflineSession(realm, sessionId);
}
{code}
there are 2 issues:
1) The boilerplate code above
2) Not good performance as under the covers, we need to query both infinispan "sessions" cache and then fallback to "offlineSessions" cache. Which could mean unecessary cluster remote calls...
This is the issue usually when session_state in the accessToken/IDToken is verified as those tokens don't have information if session was "online" or "offline" session. We may add the flag to tokens to the session_state like this for normal sessions:
"session_state": "onl/12345"
or for offline sessions:
"session_state": "off/12345"
And have some common method on UserSessionManager, which will be able to translate this session_state and lookup either "sessions" or "offlineSessions". This should help with both (1) and (2) though.
Will probably need to change accessTokens/IDTokens this way. Not sure about refresh/offline tokens as those have information if session is online or offline (token type) and changing session_state may cause some issues and additional migration effort for backwards compatibility for offline tokens.

description:
As pointed by William Burns:
{code}
Also a side note for keycloak, I would recommend adding some backpressure support at [3] - if the puts are too slow you could end up with a large amount of entries in memory at the same time.
[3] 
{code}
I think we can improve it by calling synchronous "cache.putAll" (it is synchronous, but still avoids lots of unecessary network communication). Another possibility is to remove a need for preloading entirely - this can be possible if we have a way to skip saving sessions on KC side at all and have them saved just on remote JDG server.

